
{"id":"72e959fc-a619-433b-8b7b-0836fa96b161","object":"chat.completion","created":1784985853,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    if nodes is None: raise ValueError('missing nodes')\\n    required = set(case.get('required_symbols', []))\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(n['id'])\\n    removed = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    all_ids = {n['id'] for n in nodes}\\n    def current_symbols():\\n        syms = set()\\n        for nid in all_ids:\\n            if nid not in removed:\\n                syms.add(node_map[nid]['symbol'])\\n        return syms\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid not in removed for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        removed.add(nid)\\n        syms_after = current_symbols()\\n        if required.issubset(syms_after):\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            removed.discard(nid)\\n    retained = sorted(nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in removed)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removal_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that processes a parse tree and applies a bounded objective: grammar-guided node reduction, preserving required symbols, using a specific order (depth-first deeper nodes first, tie-break by node ID). The output must be an object with `removed_node_ids` (list of strings, in order of removal) and `retained_node_ids` (list of strings, sorted). The algorithm is reminiscent of Perses syntax-guided reduction: iteratively attempt to remove removable nodes while preserving required symbols and grammar validity? But the task mentions \"grammar-valid deletion\" and \"interestingness oracle\". However, the case payload only contains nodes (with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable flag, utility) and required_symbols list. No grammar. So what does \"grammar-valid\" mean? Possibly the tree structure itself is the grammar: nodes are grammar productions, and removal is allowed only if the remaining tree is still valid (i.e., the parent-child relations preserve some grammar? Or more simply, we can only remove leaf nodes? The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies we can only remove leaf nodes, and any node that is removable and whose children (if any) are also removed first? But \"retained children\" means if a node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it. So we need to process nodes depth-first (post-order) and consider removability: removable flag must be true, not root, its symbol not in required_symbols, and its children are all already removed (or no children). Then we check if removing it still leaves all required symbols present in the remaining tree. That's the \"interestingness oracle\" – essentially a predicate that the set of symbols in the remaining tree still includes all required symbols. Since we don't have a separate oracle, we can simulate by recalculating symbols present after removal. Return removal order as the order we actually remove nodes.\n\nAlso \"registered_candidate_order\" suggests we process nodes in a specific order: by depth descending (deeper first), then by node ID as tie-break (stable). So we need to sort nodes accordingly. Then for each node in that order, check if we can remove it (removable true, not root, symbol not required, its children (all descendants?) all already removed? Actually \"node with retained children\" likely means immediate children that haven't been removed yet. But if we process depth-first descending, children are deeper, so they will be considered before their parent. So we can maintain a set of removed node IDs, and check if any of its children are not in removed set. If all children are removed or node has no children, then we can consider removing it. Also we need to verify that removing it does not cause any required symbol to be missing from the remaining tree. Since we only remove nodes one at a time, we can compute the set of symbols currently present in the tree (including all nodes not removed) and check that after removal, all required symbols are still present. Since root is never removable (removable false) and required symbols are present in root or other nodes, we need to ensure.\n\nUtility values are present but not used in the criteria description beyond maybe \"interestingness\" but we ignore them. The fixture shows utility values but no usage.\n\nSo algorithm:\n- Parse case: case is a dict with keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\".\n- Build a mapping from node id to node dict.\n- Build a mapping from parent id to list of children ids (from nodes' parent field).\n- Initialize removed set to empty.\n- Define a function symbols_present() that returns set of symbols of nodes not in removed set.\n- Get all nodes, sort by depth descending, then by id (string). Since depth is integer, deeper first means higher depth first, so descending depth.\n- For each node in sorted list:\n    - If node id is \"root\" -> skip (root has removable false anyway, but double-check).\n    - If not node[\"removable\"]: skip.\n    - If node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols: skip (required symbol nodes cannot be removed).\n    - If node has children (in children mapping) and any child id not in removed: skip (because retained children).\n    - Else, consider tentative removal:\n        - Temporarily assume removed, compute symbols_present if node removed. If all required symbols are in that set, then definitely remove:\n            - Add node id to removed set.\n            - Append node id to removal_order list.\n- After loop, retained node ids are all node ids not in removed. Sort retained node ids alphabetically (as per \"sorted retained IDs\").\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removal_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained}.\n\nBut note: The output schema requires both arrays. The retained_node_ids sorted. The removed_node_ids in removal order (the order we actually removed them, which is the order we processed them, but we only add when removed; we process depth descending, so it's consistent. Important: The tie break is node ID, which is stable (string). So we should sort nodes by ( -depth, id ) where depth descending.\n\nEdge cases: If no nodes can be removed, removed list empty, retained list all nodes sorted.\n\nCheck fixture: first case: nodes have depths 0 root, 1 n1, n2, n3, 2 n4, n5, n6. Required symbols: root, s1. root is symbol root, n1 symbol s1, n4 symbol s1, maybe others. Removable nodes: n2 (true), n4 (true), n6 (true). n2 parent root, n4 parent n2, n6 parent n1. Depth order descending: depth 2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. n5 removable false, skip. n4 removable true, symbol s1 which is required? Yes, s1 is required, so cannot remove n4. n6 removable true, symbol s0 not required, children? n6 has no children (leaf). So we can attempt remove n6. Check required symbols: root and s1. After removing n6, symbols present: root (root), s1 (n1 or n4), s2 (n2 or n5), s0 (only from n6, lost). So required symbols still present. So remove n6. Then process depth 1 nodes: n1 (removable false), n2 (removable true, symbol s2 not required), n3 (removable false). n2 has child n4, which is still present (not removed) because we didn't remove n4 (can't due to required). So n2 has retained child -